CDN — Content Delivery Network

A CDN (Content Delivery Network) is a network of servers distributed across different geographical locations that delivers content
to users from a server close to them.

Simple example

Suppose your Spring Boot application is hosted in Germany, but a user is in Pakistan.

Without CDN:

User (Pakistan)
      |
      | HTTP Request
      ↓
Server (Germany)
      |
      ↓
Response

Every request travels to Germany → higher latency.

With CDN:

                    ┌── CDN Server (Pakistan)
                    │
User (Pakistan) ────┤
                    │
                    └── Origin Server (Germany)

The CDN can serve cached content from a nearby server.

What does a CDN usually cache?

Mostly static content:

Images
CSS
JavaScript
Videos
Fonts
PDFs
Static HTML
Sometimes API responses

For example:

https://example.com/images/logo.png

First request:

User → CDN → Origin Server
              ↓
          logo.png
              ↓
          CDN caches it

Next users:

User → CDN → logo.png

The origin server doesn't need to handle every request.

CDN vs your backend

Imagine you have:

React/Angular frontend
        ↓
      CDN
        ↓
Spring Boot APIs
        ↓
      MySQL

The CDN is mainly responsible for fast delivery of cacheable content.

Your Spring Boot application handles:

POST /login
POST /payment
GET /customer
POST /order

while CDN can handle things like:

GET /app.js
GET /style.css
GET /logo.png
GET /product-image.jpg
How does CDN know where to get content?

There are two important concepts:

1. Origin Server

Your actual server:

Spring Boot Server
        ↑
   Origin Server
2. Edge Server

CDN's geographically distributed server:

Pakistan Edge
Dubai Edge
Singapore Edge
London Edge
US Edge
...

The user is routed to an appropriate edge location.

Cache HIT vs Cache MISS

This is an important interview concept.

Cache HIT

Content already exists in CDN:

User
 ↓
CDN
 ↓
Cached image

Very fast.

Cache MISS

Content isn't cached:

User
 ↓
CDN
 ↓
Origin Server
 ↓
CDN caches response
 ↓
User

The next request can become a cache hit.

Why use CDN?

Main benefits:

1. Lower latency

User gets content from a geographically closer server.

2. Reduced backend load

Instead of:

100,000 users
      ↓
Spring Boot

you can have:

100,000 users
      ↓
     CDN
      ↓
   only some requests
      ↓
Spring Boot
3. Better scalability

CDN absorbs a large amount of traffic.

4. DDoS protection

Many CDN providers also provide traffic filtering and DDoS protection.

5. Faster static content

Images, JS, CSS, videos etc. can be served much faster.

Interview answer

If interviewer asks "What is a CDN?", you can say:

A CDN is a geographically distributed network of edge servers that caches and delivers content closer to users.
It reduces latency, decreases load on the origin server, improves scalability, and can also provide features like DDoS protection and TLS termination.

One important distinction

CDN does not replace your backend.

Think:

             ┌── Static content
             │
User → CDN ──┤
             │
             └── API → Load Balancer → Spring Boot → DB

The CDN is primarily the fast delivery/cache layer, while your Spring Boot services remain responsible for business logic.